    {
      "role": "duck",
      "text": "You were looking into that timeout in the order sync retries test. Why are you only seeing two events instead of three?"
    },
    {
      "role": "you",
      "text": "I've got an integration test and go that times out sometimes and passes other times. It waits for three sync events but only gets two."
    },
    {
      "role": "duck",
      "text": "Intermittent failures. Since it passes sometimes, what's different about the runs that fail?"
    },
    {
      "role": "you",
      "text": "It fails maybe one run in three, I changed the retry logic and the worker just before it started."
    },
    {
      "role": "duck",
      "text": "You changed the retry logic and the worker. What specific part of that logic controls when a sync event is emitted?"
    },
    {
      "role": "you",
      "text": "I haven't looked at ordering, the test might be checking before the third event has been sent."
    },
    {
      "role": "duck",
      "text": "The test might be checking too early. If you increased the wait time, would the third event eventually arrive?"
    },
    {
      "role": "you",
      "text": "I suppose I could add a log line with timestamps when each event is sent and when the test checks."
    },
    {
      "role": "duck",
      "text": "Adding timestamps for the events and the check. What would those logs prove about the timing?"
    },
    {
      "role": "you",
      "text": "Okay, so if the check happens first it's a race and the test, and if not it's the worker dropping an event."
    },
    {
      "role": "duck",
      "text": "A race or a dropped event. Which of those two possibilities would you try to rule out first?"
    },
    {
      "role": "you",
      "text": "and walk."
    },
    {
      "role": "duck",
      "text": "Okay, ending the walk. Nice thinking out there."
    }
